nodecode-openrouter-voice is a rewrite of esketids's esketids/hermes-openrouter-voice:

https://github.com/esketids/hermes-openrouter-voice
commit 3f2478ab6e795da54547e7a4b162dd207d73079a
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 esketids (LICENSE)

What was rewritten is the whole of it, from Python to Common Lisp against
Nodecode's cell contract. The two OpenRouter endpoints, the multipart
transcription request with `response_format` json, the mp3 speech request,
the vendor-prefixed slug fallback, the 16 kHz mono WAV normaliser (downmix
and linear resample, written by hand as the original does), the rate split
between what a model honours itself and an `ffmpeg atempo` chain, the shipped
model catalogs and the rule that a model publishing no voices is sent
without one: all carried over. The original's plugin scaffolding, its
`stt.providers` / `tts.providers` command shims and the desktop GUI patch it
documents are left out, since Nodecode has no voice-provider seam for them to
fill: the same two directions arrive as the tools `transcribe_audio` and
`speak_text` and the command `/voice`. Also left out: its `~/.hermes/.env`
lookup (the key comes from the environment or the section), its profile
config discovery, and the `models.py` catalog CLI, whose data now lives in
the cell and whose live diff would be one more network path to keep.
